n_009) making the net pathway non-viable until fixed.', 'P8 (Successive Strecker with chiral crystallization) reproduces the balance flaws seen in its Strecker network analogs. Chiral selection step alone is plausible, overall route is not, as written.', 'P9 (Direct amination of propanoic acid/lactamization) is hindered by rxn_002 and rxn_003 stoichiometry errors but otherwise plausible in mineral-catalyzed prebiotic settings.', 'P10 (Aldehyde pathway with surface cycling) has multiple unbalanced carbon and nitrogen transfers (rxn_010, rxn_011, rxn_009). Requires balancing and possibly detail of facilitating mineral species.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.72\n- Feedback: The network captures a rich and logical range of prebiotic scenarios and chemical steps for L-proline synthesis. However, network validity and chemical feasibility are critically compromised by pervasive atom/charge balance failures at almost every step outside the chain extension. Several intermediates also have structural or formula inconsistencies (notably with RDKit parsing failures). Environmental assignments and progression are credible throughout and the strategies are creative, but the network cannot be approved until each reaction conserves mass and charge, and all intermediates are clearly defined and matched to their formulas. Major revision is needed to correct stoichiometry, complete unbalanced or ambiguous steps, and ensure every transformation is chemically rigorous. Literature alignment cannot be meaningfully scored in the absence of balanced, mechanistically specified reactions.\n- Issues: ['P1 includes a series of unbalanced reactions (rxn_001\u2013rxn_004). Key intermediates are identified and the pathway is logical, but mass balance failures throughout (except rxn_005, which is reasonable) harm chemical feasibility. Environmental assignments largely make sense. Correction of atom balances and some mechanistic steps is needed.', 'This pathway leverages plausible surface reactions but fails atom/charge balance at the Strecker, dehydration, and cyclization steps. Reactant selection is logical and steps are in a plausible prebiotic order. Correction of chemical formulas and product accounting is required for higher feasibility.', 'Short, plausible route but undermined by mass imbalances in key forming steps (rxn_006, rxn_008). The progression to proline is sensible but chemically incomplete without full atom conservation. Propose balancing all reactions and clarify any ambiguous cyclization chemistry.', 'Environmental and step logic are strong, but rxn_010 and rxn_004 are unbalanced (C-1 in amination, formula mismatch in cyclization). The pathway is attractive but not viable without mass/charge conservation.', 'Cyclization and amination are plausible but mass imbalance (C deficit in rxn_010) remains. Some steps are well conceived prebiotically. Proline can be accessed in theory, but the mass conservation issue is a critical defect.', 'This alternative outlines logical steps but rxn_012, rxn_002, rxn_003, and rxn_004 are all mass/charge unbalanced. Pathway structure is reasonable; however, the atom-conservation failures are too significant for approval.', 'Alanine reduction and subsequent cyclizations are conceptually correct but lack full mass conservation and include species with ambiguous formulas. Some steps have viable analogues in the literature but are incomplete as written.', 'Chain extension via maleic acid is nearly balanced (rxn_009) but the subsequent steps have minor mass deficits. Still, structurally this pathway is sensible and the main flaw is incomplete accounting for all atoms.', 'Chemical routes from propanal are underdeveloped (rxn_015 missing), and mass/charge balance is not achieved. Correcting all steps is necessary for viability.', 'Exogenous CO2 as a cyclization agent is a conceptually reasonable path, but C atom imbalance in rxn_010 is persistent. With correct mass balance, this pathway would become competitive.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Proline\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network employs multiple convergent strategies characteristic of prebiotic chemistries: (1) hydrothermal and surface environments to generate 4-aminobutanoic acid (GABA) and its aldehyde/deaminated analogs; (2) Strecker-like homologation, reductive amination, and multistep chain extension to reach the C4-amine backbone; (3) mineral surface catalysis, especially by iron sulfides and clays, to promote carbon-nitrogen bond formation and ring closure; (4) ring-closing lactam cyclizations under dehydration conditions to yield proline. Mechanistically, the network is built around well-supported transformations (Strecker synthesis, reductive amination, hydrothermal carbonyl chemistry, and surface-facilitated cyclizations), arranged in ten distinct yet intersecting pathways to maximize chemical plausibility and atom balance.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h3,5H,1-2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"GABA\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO2/c5-3-1-2-4(6)7/h1-3,5H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobut-2-enoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H7NO2/c5-3-1-2-4(6)7/h2-3,5H,1H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h4H,1-3,5H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"L-proline\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c6-4-2-1-3-5(7)8/h1-4,6H2,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"propanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"propanal\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-2-3-4/h3H,2H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-proline (racemic)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c6-4-2-1-3-5(7)8/h1-4,6H2,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"butyric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O2/c1-2-3-4(5)6/h2-3H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"mackinawite (FeS)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"mackinawite (FeS)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Moderate pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde reacts with HCN and ammonia to form aminonitrile, which is hydrolyzed to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Strecker synthesis is well-documented in hydrothermal prebiotic settings (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 1998; Bada, 2013). FeS facilitates nitrile hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Amino acid chain extension via aldehyde and Strecker homologation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycle\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycine reacts with formaldehyde (as a carbon source) under Strecker conditions, extending the chain to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stepwise Strecker-type homologation has been established for C1 to C2, and C2 to C3 amino acid extension (Patel et al., 2015). Montmorillonite enhances the local concentration and stability of transition states.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Transamination of propanal to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Propanal undergoes amination with ammonia, with magnetite catalyzing conversion to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported in origin-of-life studies simulating primitive enzymatic functions by iron oxides (Kitadai et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of propanal to 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite (Fe3S4)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-450K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite (Fe3S4)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde reacts with ammonia and H2, catalyzed by FeS mineral, to yield primary amine and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"FeS cluster surfaces are known reductive catalysts, enabling amination of carbonyls (Muchowska et al., 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of butanoic acid to 4-aminobutanoic acid (GABA)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite (Fe3S4)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-450K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite (Fe3S4)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination of butanoic acid at the gamma position leads to GABA and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotic amination of carboxylic acids by surface catalysis is demonstrated in iron-sulfur systems (Kitadai et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol extension of propanal to butanoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Propanal + formaldehyde (aldol) + CO2 + water \u2192 butanoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldol and carboxylation processes mediated by Fe-minerals are widely regarded as plausible (Cody et al., 2000).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidation of 3-aminopropanal to GABA\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV exposure and/or metal oxide surfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminated aldehyde is oxidized (via CO2) to carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Non-enzymatic aldehyde oxidations occur in wet-dry cycles, especially with UV and trace minerals (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydration cyclization of GABA to proline (lactamization)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dehydrative ring closure (lactamization) under partial dehydration yields proline and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Clay-catalyzed lactamizations under evaporative conditions are experimentally demonstrated for several amino acid homologs (Danger et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydrogenation of GABA to 4-aminobut-2-enoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"hematite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hematite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Surface UV\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxidative dehydrogenation converts GABA to unsaturated aminocarboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Photocatalysis on hematite surfaces is a plausible mechanism for C\u2013H dehydrogenation (Abelson, 1966; speculative step for network diversity)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of 4-aminobut-2-enoic acid to proline (unsaturated route)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Drying (evaporation)\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactamization after C=C isomerization and hydration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thermal conditions and mild dehydration support this mode of cyclization. It is a speculative alternative with plausible atom flow.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidative deamination of alanine to propanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"hematite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hematite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Surface UV\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alanine oxidatively loses ammonia, generating propanal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxidative deamination is supported in UV/iron oxide settings (Kitadai et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of 4-aminobutanal to proline (direct cyclization)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct cyclization (lactam formation) of aminoaldehydes to five-membered ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldehyde-amino cyclization to pyrrolidone rings is established under mild aqueous conditions (Weber & Pizzarello, 2006); requires prior aminoaldehyde formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidation of 4-aminobutanol to GABA\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"hematite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hematite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxidation of aminoalcohol or aldehyde yields GABA.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"In the presence of hematite and water, robust C=O oxidation is plausible (Abelson, 1966; Sutherland, 2016).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Homo-Strecker homologation (GABA from glycine via C2 extension)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Chain-elongated Strecker synthesis from glycine to GABA, via aminonitrile intermediate and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Extensions beyond the canonical amino acids have been explored in model prebiotic systems (Patel et al., 2015; Danger et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Racemic proline generation via non-selective lactamization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Evaporitic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dry cyclization yields both enantiomers (DL-proline) and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Silica-catalyzed prebiotic cyclizations are known to be unselective; step provides non-chiral proline for potential chiral resolution downstream (Meierhenrich et al., 2005).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Chiral resolution of DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"chiral hydrophobic amino acids (templating)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"primitive chiral amino acids\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Interface or pool\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Kinetic or crystallization-based selection of the L-enantiomer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chiral selection by precipitation/crystallization is described in prebiotic and meteorite studies (Soai et al., 2003; Pizzarello & Weber, 2010).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"GABA route via aldehyde amination and cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal and surface chain extension from formaldehyde via glycine and alanine to propanal, aminated to GABA, lactamized to proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Butanoic acid amination pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Aldol-like chain extension to butanoic acid, followed by reductive amination to GABA and cyclization to L-proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct cyclization of aminoaldehyde pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Propanal is aminated to 3-aminopropanal, chain-elongated to 4-aminobutanal and directly cyclized into proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Unsaturated GABA and cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"GABA is dehydrogenated and unsaturated intermediate cyclizes (after hydration) to L-proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Double Strecker homologation to GABA, then proline\",\n      \"description\": \"Two Strecker steps from glycine to GABA, then lactamization/cyclization to proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Racemic proline and chiral resolution\",\n      \"description\": \"Racemic proline is formed by nonselective lactamization of GABA, with subsequent chiral resolution for L-form.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidative conversion of aminoaldehyde to GABA, then proline\",\n      \"description\": \"Aminoaldehyde is oxidized to GABA, then cyclized to proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Transamination-oxidation to propanal pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Alanine undergoes transamination to form propanal, feeds into two proline-producing routes.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Chain-elongation via surface-catalyzed Strecker\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface Strecker chemistry yields longer chain amino acids, which then produce L-proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Silica-catalyzed DL-proline with chiral enrichment\",\n      \"description\": \"Prebiotic racemization upon cyclization, with spontaneous L-proline enrichment by crystallization/templating.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_014\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "This network employs multiple convergent strategies characteristic of prebiotic chemistries: (1) hydrothermal and surface environments to generate 4-aminobutanoic acid (GABA) and its aldehyde/deaminated analogs; (2) Strecker-like homologation, reductive amination, and multistep chain extension to reach the C4-amine backbone; (3) mineral surface catalysis, especially by iron sulfides and clays, to promote carbon-nitrogen bond formation and ring closure; (4) ring-closing lactam cyclizations under dehydration conditions to yield proline. Mechanistically, the network is built around well-supported transformations (Strecker synthesis, reductive amination, hydrothermal carbonyl chemistry, and surface-facilitated cyclizations), arranged in ten distinct yet intersecting pathways to maximize chemical plausibility and atom balance.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2CO", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanal", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h3,5H,1-2,4H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H9NO2", "iupac_name": "4-aminobutanoic acid", "common_name": "GABA", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9NO2/c5-3-1-2-4(6)7/h1-3,5H2,(H,6,7)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H7NO2", "iupac_name": "4-aminobut-2-enoic acid", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NO2/c5-3-1-2-4(6)7/h2-3,5H,1H2,(H,6,7)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H7NO", "iupac_name": "4-aminobutanal", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9NO/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h4H,1-3,5H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "L-proline", "common_name": "L-proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c6-4-2-1-3-5(7)8/h1-4,6H2,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_na