ino acid chain extension via aldehyde and Strecker homologation", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_002", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Wet-dry cycle"}, "mechanism": "Glycine reacts with formaldehyde (as a carbon source) under Strecker conditions, extending the chain to alanine.", "reasoning": "Stepwise Strecker-type homologation has been established for C1 to C2, and C2 to C3 amino acid extension (Patel et al., 2015). Montmorillonite enhances the local concentration and stability of transition states."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Transamination of propanal to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Propanal undergoes amination with ammonia, with magnetite catalyzing conversion to alanine.", "reasoning": "Supported in origin-of-life studies simulating primitive enzymatic functions by iron oxides (Kitadai et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Reductive amination of propanal to 3-aminopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["greigite (Fe3S4)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "400-450K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "greigite (Fe3S4)", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Aldehyde reacts with ammonia and H2, catalyzed by FeS mineral, to yield primary amine and water.", "reasoning": "FeS cluster surfaces are known reductive catalysts, enabling amination of carbonyls (Muchowska et al., 2020)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Amination of butanoic acid to 4-aminobutanoic acid (GABA)", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_002", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["greigite (Fe3S4)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "400-450K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "greigite (Fe3S4)", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Reductive amination of butanoic acid at the gamma position leads to GABA and water.", "reasoning": "Prebiotic amination of carboxylic acids by surface catalysis is demonstrated in iron-sulfur systems (Kitadai et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Aldol extension of propanal to butanoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001", "mol_012", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Propanal + formaldehyde (aldol) + CO2 + water \u2192 butanoic acid.", "reasoning": "Aldol and carboxylation processes mediated by Fe-minerals are widely regarded as plausible (Cody et al., 2000)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Oxidation of 3-aminopropanal to GABA", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_012", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV exposure and/or metal oxide surfaces"}, "mechanism": "Aminated aldehyde is oxidized (via CO2) to carboxylic acid.", "reasoning": "Non-enzymatic aldehyde oxidations occur in wet-dry cycles, especially with UV and trace minerals (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Dehydration cyclization of GABA to proline (lactamization)", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite", "aqueous": false, "other": "Wet-dry cycling"}, "mechanism": "Dehydrative ring closure (lactamization) under partial dehydration yields proline and water.", "reasoning": "C